<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_imp_and_inv" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Derivatives of Inverse Functions and Implicit Differentiation</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
                    One important application of derivatives of compositions of functions is finding derivatives of functions that are defined only indirectly in terms of other functions. For example, all we know about <m>\arcsin(x)</m> is that it is the inverse of (the principle branch of) the sine. So, how do we find its derivative?
    </p>
    <p>
            Another instance of this kind of idea is found in progress check 1 where we are given the formula
            <md>V=8(h-3)\sqrt{9-\left(h-3\right)^{2}}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{h-3}{3}\right)+36\pi</md>
                    for the volume of a water tank (in cubic meters) as a function of the depth of the water (in meters), and we also find the volume as a function of time to be given by <m>V=1.1t\text{m}^{3}</m>, where <m>t</m> is given in minutes. In this case, we know from our physical experience that the depth of the water is a function of time, but there is no way to directly solve for <m>h</m> as a function of <m>t</m>. Nevertheless, we can use ideas from derivatives of compositions of functions to calculate the rate of change of the depth as a function of time.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection>
          <title>Practice with Derivatives of Compositions</title>
          <example>
                  <title>The Derivative of a Composition</title>
                  <statement>
                          <p>
                                          We will rewrite <m>f(x)=\sqrt{x^{2}+4}</m> as a composition of functions and find its derivative.
                          </p>
                          <p>
                                          Let <m>y=g(x)=x^{2}+4</m> and let <m>z=h(y)=\sqrt{y}</m>. Thus <m>z=h(y)=h(g(x))</m>, or equivalently <m>z=\sqrt{y}=\sqrt{x^{2}+4}</m>.
                          </p>
                          <p>
                                  Using Leibniz's notation, we know that <m>\dd{z}{x}=\dd{z}{y}\dd{y}{x}</m>. Calculating <m>\dd{z}{y}</m> and <m>\dd{y}{x}</m>, we have
                                  <md>
                                          <mrow>
                                                  \dd{z}{x}
                                                          &amp;=\dd{z}{y}\dd{y}{x}
                                          </mrow>
                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\dd{{}\sqrt{y}}{y}\dd{(x^{2}+4)}{x}
                                          </mrow>
                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\dd{{}y^{1/2}}{y}\dd{(x^{2}+4)}{x}
                                          </mrow>
                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\left(\frac{1}{2}y^{-1/2}\right)\left(2x\right)
                                          </mrow>
                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\left(\frac{1}{2\sqrt{y}}\right)\left(2x\right)
                                          </mrow>
                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\left(\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x^2+4}}\right)\left(2x\right)
                                          </mrow>
                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\frac{2x}{2\sqrt{x^2+4}}
                                          </mrow>
                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+4}}
                                          </mrow>
                                  </md>
                          </p>
                          <p>
                                  Alternatively, we know that <m>\left(h(g(x))\right)'=h'(g(x))g'(x)</m>. Now, <m>h(y)=\sqrt{y}=y^{1/2}</m>so <m>h'(y)=\frac{1}{2}y^{-1/2}=1/(2\sqrt{y})</m>, and <m>g(x)=x^{2}+4</m>, so <m>g'(x)=2x</m>. Therefore, 
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \left(\sqrt{x^{2}+4}\right)'
                                                          &amp;=\left(h(g(x))\right)'
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=h'(g(x))g'(x)
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{g(x)}}\left(g'(x)\right)
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x^{2}+4}}\left(2x\right)
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{2x}{2\sqrt{x^{2}+4}}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^{2}+4}}.
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                          </p>
                  </statement>
          </example>
          <exercises>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Find the derivative of <m>\sqrt{x^{3}+6x}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Find the derivative of <m>\sin(x^{3}+6x)</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Find the derivative of <m>\sqrt{\sin(x)}</m> (assuming <m>\sin(x)\gt 0</m>).
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Find the derivative of <m>\nexp{x^{3}+6x}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Let <m>g(x)</m> be an unspecified function. How would you find the derivative of <m>\sqrt{g(x)}</m>?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Let <m>g(x)</m> be an unspecified function. How would you find the derivative of <m>g\big(\sin(x)\big)</m>?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
          </exercises>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
          <title>Derivatives of Inverse Functions</title>
          <example>
                  <statement>
                          <p>
                                          We will use composition of functions to find the derivative of <m>\arcsin(x)</m>. First of all, we know that <m>\arcsin(x)</m> is the inverse function of the principle branch of the sine. That is, <m>\sin(\theta)</m> with the domain restricted to <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}\le \theta\le \frac{\pi}{2}</m>. Thus, for any <m>x</m> with <m>-1\le x\le 1</m>, if <m>\theta=\arcsin(x)</m>, then <m>\sin(\theta)=x</m> and <m>-\frac{\pi}{2}\le \theta\le \frac{\pi}{2}</m>. In particular, for any <m>x</m> with <m>-1\le x\le 1</m>,
                                          <md>
                                                  \sin(\arcsin(x))=x.
                                          </md>
                          </p>
                          <p>
                                          Let <m>-1\le x\le 1</m>, let <m>y=\arcsin(x)</m>, and let <m>z=\sin(y)</m>. We will now calculate <m>\dd{z}{x}</m> in two different ways, and then use the fact that the derivative of <m>z</m> must be the same no matter which way we calculate it.
                                  </p>
                                  <p>
                                                  First, we know that <m>\dd{z}{x}=\dd{z}{y}\dd{y}{x}</m>. But 
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>\dd{z}{y}&amp;=\dd{{}\sin(y)}{y}</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>         &amp;=\cos(y).</mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                                          Then, we have
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>\dd{z}{x}&amp;=\dd{z}{y}\dd{y}{x}</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>         &amp;=\dd{{}\sin(y)}{y}\dd{y}{x}</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>         &amp;=\cos(y)\dd{y}{x}</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>         &amp;=\cos\big(\arcsin(x)\big)\dd{{}\arcsin(x)}{x}.</mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                                          The value of <m>\dd{{}\arcsin(x)}{x}</m> is what we want to find.
                                  </p>
                                  <p>
                                                  Next, we know that <m>z=\sin\left(\arcsin(x)\right)=x</m>, so <m>\dd{z}{x}=\dd{x}{x}=1.</m>
                                  </p>
                                  <p>
                                                  Finally, from trigonometry, we know that if <m>\sin(\theta)=x</m> and <m>-\pi/2\le \theta\le\pi/2</m>, then <m>\cos(\theta)\ge 0</m>, and so <m>\cos(\theta)=\sqrt{1-x^{2}}</m>. Thus, because <m>-\pi/2\le\arcsin(x)\le\pi/2</m> and <m>\sin(\arcsin(x))=x</m>, <m>\cos(\arcsin(x))=\sqrt{1-x^{2}}</m>. Putting all of this together, we have
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>\dd{z}{x}&amp;=\dd{z}{y}\dd{y}{x}</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>         &amp;=\cos\big(\arcsin(x)\big)\dd{{}\arcsin(x)}{x}.</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>         &amp;=\sqrt{1-x^{2}}\,\dd{{}\arcsin(x)}{x}</mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                                  and
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>\dd{z}{x}&amp;=\dd{x}{x}</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>         &amp;=1</mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                                          so <m>\sqrt{1-x^{2}}\,\dd{{}\arcsin(x)}{x}=1</m>, and therefore, for any <m>-1\lt x\lt 1</m>,
                                                  <md>
                                                          \dd{{}\arcsin(x)}{x}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}.
                                                  </md>
                                  </p>
                  </statement>
          </example>
          <p>
                  More generally, we have the following theorem.
          </p>
          <theorem>
                  <title>Derivatives of Inverse Functions</title>
                  <statement>
                          <p>
                          For any differentiable, invertible function <m>f</m>, with inverse <m>f^{-1}</m>, for any <m>x</m> for which <m>f'\left(f^{-1}(x)\right)\neq 0</m>, 
                          <md>
                                  \dd{{}f^{-1}(x)}{x}=\frac{1}{f'\left(f^{-1}(x)\right)}.
                          </md>
                          </p>
                  </statement>
          </theorem>
          <exercises>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Use the facts that for any <m>-\pi/2\lt x\lt \pi/2</m>, <m>\tan(\arctan(x))=x</m> and <m>\dd{{}\tan(x)}{x}=1/\cos^{2}(x)</m> to find the derivative of <m>\arctan(x)</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
                  <exercise>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Use the fact that for any <m>x\gt 0</m>, <m>\nexp{\ln(x)}=x</m> to find the derivative of <m>\ln(x)</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </exercise>
                  <exercise>
                          <introduction>
                                  <p>
                                          Hyperbolic cosine (<m>\cosh(x)</m>) and hyperbolic sine (<m>\sinh(x)</m>), which occur frequently in engineering applications, are defined by
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \cosh(x)&amp;=\frac{\nexp{x}+\nexp{-x}}{2}
                                                                  &amp;
                                                          \sinh(x)&amp;=\frac{\nexp{x}-\nexp{-x}}{2}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </introduction>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Calculate <m>\sinh(0)</m> and <m>\cosh(0)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Show that the derivative of <m>\sinh(x)</m> is <m>\cosh(x)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Show that the derivative of <m>\cosh(x)</m> is <m>\sinh(x)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <introduction>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Verify that for any real number, <m>x</m>:
                                                  </p>
                                          </introduction>
                                          <task>
                                                  <statement>
                                                          <p>
                                                                  <m>\cosh(x)+\sinh(x)=\nexp{x}.</m>
                                                          </p>
                                                  </statement>
                                          </task>
                                          <task>
                                                  <statement>
                                                          <p>
                                                                  <m>\cosh(x)-\sinh(x)=\nexp{-x}.</m>
                                                          </p>
                                                  </statement>
                                          </task>
                                          <task>
                                                  <statement>
                                                          <p>
                                                                  <m>\cosh^{2}(x)-\sinh^{2}(x)=1.</m>
                                                          </p>
                                                  </statement>
                                          </task>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Explain why for every real <m>x</m>, <m>\sinh(x)</m> is strictly increasing at <m>x</m>, and therefore has an inverse function, <m>\arcsinh(x)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Show that for any real numbers, <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, if <m>\sinh(y)=x</m> then <m>\cosh(y)=\sqrt{1+x^{2}}</m> and therefore <m>\cosh(\arcsinh(x))=\sqrt{1+x^{2}}</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Let <m>f(x)=\arcsinh(x)</m>. Calculate <m>f'(0)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  Let <m>f(x)=\arcsinh(x)</m>. Calculate <m>f'(x)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                                  </task>
                  </exercise>
          </exercises>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
          <title>Derivative of Implicitly Defined Functions</title>
          <example>
                  <statement>
          <p>
                          Let the volume of a water tank (in cubic meters) as a function of depth of the water (in meters) be given by
                  <md>V=8(h-3)\sqrt{9-\left(h-3\right)^{2}}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{h-3}{3}\right)+36\pi</md>
                          and let water be flowing into the (empty) tank at a rate of <m>1.1\text{m}^{3}/\text{min}</m>. We will find the rate at which the water is rising when the water is <m>2\text{m}</m> deep.
                 
          </p>
          <p>
                          Although it is not given explicitly, we know that the depth of the water is a function of time. Furthermore, there is no way to solve for <m>h</m> as a function of time. Thus, we will treat <m>h</m> as an unspecified <term>implicit function</term>.
          </p>
          <p>
                  From our work with derivatives of compositions, we know that 
                  <md>\dd{V}{t}=\dd{V}{h}\dd{h}{t}.</md>
                  To calculate <m>\dd{V}{h}</m>, first we will let <m>k=h-3</m>, so that 
                  <md>
                          V=8k\sqrt{9-k^{2}}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{k}{3}\right)+36\pi.
                  </md>
                          Furthermore, <m>\dd{k}{h}=\dd{{}(h-3)}{h}=1</m>, so
                  <md>
                          <mrow> \dd{V}{h} &amp;=\dd{V}{k}\cdot\dd{k}{h} </mrow>
                          <mrow>           &amp;=\dd{V}{k}\cdot 1 </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=\dd{{}\left(8k\sqrt{9-k^{2}}\right)}{k}+\dd{{}\left(72\arcsin\left(\frac{k}{3}\right)\right)}{k}+\dd{{}36\pi}{k}
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=
                          \left(8k\dd{{}\left(\sqrt{9-k^{2}}\right)}{k}
                          +\dd{{}\left(8k\right)}{k}\sqrt{9-k^{2}}\right)
                          +72\left(
                          \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{k}{3}\right)^{2}}}
                          \right)\dd{{}\left(\frac{k}{3}\right)}{k}
                          +0
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=
                          \left(8k\frac{1}{2\sqrt{9-k^{2}}}\dd{9-k^{2}}{k}
                          +8\sqrt{9-k^{2}}\right)
                          +72\left(
                          \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{k}{3}\right)^{2}}}
                          \right)\frac{1}{3}
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=
                          \left(8k\frac{1}{2\sqrt{9-k^{2}}}(-2k)
                          +8\sqrt{9-k^{2}}\right)
                          +72\left(
                          \frac{3}{\sqrt{9-{k}^{2}}}
                          \right)\frac{1}{3}
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=
                          8\left(\frac{-k^{2}}{\sqrt{9-k^{2}}}
                          +\sqrt{9-k^{2}}\right)
                          +\left(
                          \frac{72}{\sqrt{9-{k}^{2}}}
                          \right)
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=
                          8\left(\frac{-k^{2}}{\sqrt{9-k^{2}}}
                          +\sqrt{9-k^{2}}
                          + \frac{9}{\sqrt{9-{k}^{2}}}
                          \right)
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=
                          8\left(\frac{9-k^{2}}{\sqrt{9-k^{2}}}
                          +\sqrt{9-k^{2}}
                          \right)
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;=
                          8\left({\sqrt{9-k^{2}}}
                          +\sqrt{9-k^{2}}
                          \right)
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;= 16 {\sqrt{9-k^{2}}}
                          </mrow>
                          <mrow>  
                                          &amp;= 16 {\sqrt{9-(h-3)^{2}}}
                          </mrow>
                  </md>
                  Now, we also know that <m>\dd{V}{t}=1.1</m>, so
                  <md>
                          <mrow> 1.1&amp;=\dd{V}{t} </mrow>
                          <mrow>    &amp;=\dd{V}{h}\dd{h}{t} </mrow>
                          <mrow>    &amp;=16 {\sqrt{9-(h-3)^{2}}}\dd{h}{t} </mrow>
                  </md>
                  and so
                  <md>
                          <mrow>\dd{h}{t}&amp;=\frac{1.1}{16 {\sqrt{9-(h-3)^{2}}}}.</mrow>
                  </md>
          </p>
          <p>
                          Finally,  we evaluate this at <m>h=2</m>:
                          <md>
                                  <mrow>\left.\dd{h}{t}\right\rvert_{h=2}&amp;=\frac{1.1}{16 {\sqrt{9-(2-3)^{2}}}}</mrow>
                                  <mrow>&amp;=\frac{1.1}{16 {\sqrt{9-(-1)^{2}}}}</mrow>
                                  <mrow>&amp;=\frac{1.1}{16 {\sqrt{8}}}</mrow>
                                  <mrow>&amp;=\frac{1.1}{32 {\sqrt{2}}}</mrow>
                                  <mrow>&amp;\approx 0.243067956\text{m}/\text{min}.</mrow>
                          </md>
          </p>
                  </statement>
          </example>
          <example>
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  We will find the equation of the tangent line to the ellipse given by <m>13x^{2}+10xy+13y^{2}=36</m> at the point <m>\left(0,\frac{6\sqrt{13}}{13}\right)</m>. (As shown in <xref ref="fig_ellipse"/>.)
                          </p>
                          <figure xml:id="fig_ellipse" >
                                  <caption>An Ellipse and a Tangent Line</caption>
                                  <image width="50%">
                                          <description>
                                                  <p>
                                                          The ellipse <m>13x^{2}+10xy+13y^{2}=36</m> and the tangent line to the ellipse at <m>\left(0,\frac{6\sqrt{13}}{13}\right)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </description>
                                          <xi:include href="./Ellipse.xml" />
                                  </image>
                          </figure>
                  </introduction>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  First, we will verify that the point <m>\left(0,\frac{6\sqrt{13}}{13}\right)</m> is a point on the graph of <m>13x^{2}+10xy+13y^{2}=36</m> by substituting the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates into the equation to see if they make the equation true.
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  13(0)^{2}+10(0)\left(\frac{6\sqrt{13}}{13}\right)+13\left(\frac{6\sqrt{13}}{13}\right)^{2}&amp;=13\left(\frac{6\sqrt{13}}{13}\right)^{2}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=13\left(\frac{36\cdot{13}}{13\cdot 13}\right)
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=36
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                                  so we see that the point is on the graph.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Next, we will find the <term>differential</term> of the left hand side of the equation. We do this by taking the derivative, treating every variable as a function, and ending with the operator <m>\di{}</m> applied to every variable. We will then collect <m>\di{x}</m> and <m>\di{y}</m>, treating them like variables.
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  \di{\left(13x^{2}+10xy+13y^{2}\right)}
                                                                  &amp;=
                                                                  13\di{\left(x^{2}\right)}
                                                                  +10\di{\left(xy\right)}+13\di{\left(y^{2}\right)}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=
                                                                  13\cdot2x\di{x}
                                                                  +10\left(x\di{y}+\di{x}y\right)
                                                                  +13\cdot2y\di y 
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=
                                                                  26x\di{x}
                                                                  +10x\di{y}+10y\di{x}
                                                                  +26y\di{y} 
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=
                                                                  (26x+10y)\di{x}+(10x +26y)\di{y} 
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Now, since the derivative of a constant is zero, <m>\di{(36)}=0</m>, and so we have <m>(26x+10y)\di{x}+(10x +26y)\di{y}=0</m>. We will now isolate <m>\frac{\di{y}}{\di{x}}</m>:
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  (26x+10y)\di{x}+(10x +26y)\di{y}=0
                                                                  &amp;\to
                                                                  (10x +26y)\di{y}=-(26x+10y)\di{x}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\to
                                                                  \frac{\di{y}}{\di{x}}=\frac{-(26x+10y)}{(10x +26y)}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          We now calculate <m>\left.\dd{y}{x}\right\rvert_{(x,y)=(0,6\sqrt{13}/{13})}</m> to find the slope of the tangent line.
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  \left.\dd{y}{x}\right\rvert_{(x,y)=(0,6\sqrt{13}/{13})}
                                                                          &amp;=
                                                                  \left.\frac{-(26x+10y)}{(10x +26y)}\right\rvert_{(x,y)=(0,6\sqrt{13}/{13})}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                          &amp;=
                                                                  \frac{-(26(0)+10(6\sqrt{13}/13))}{(10(0) +26(6\sqrt{13}/13))}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                          &amp;=
                                                                  \frac{-10(6\sqrt{13}/13)}{26(6\sqrt{13}/13)}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                          &amp;=
                                                                  \frac{-5}{13}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Finally, we will use the point-slope form of a line to find the equation of the tangent line: <m>y=\frac{-5}{13}(x-0)+\frac{6\sqrt{13}}{13}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
          </example>
          <exercise>
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  A train is travelling due east at <m>90\text{m}/\text{s}</m> (approximately <m>201.324\text{mph}</m>). An observer is positioned <m>100\text{m}</m> south of a point, <m>P</m>, on the track and is following the train as it approaches from the west with a camera mounted on a tripod.
                          </p>
                          <p>
                                          We want to know the rate at which the camera is turning (in radians per second) when the train is <m>50\text{m}</m> west of <m>P</m>.
                          </p>
                  </introduction>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          Let <m>x</m> be the displacement of the train relative to <m>P</m> (with west being negative and east being positive) and let <m>\theta</m> be the angle that the line-of-sight of the camera makes with the line from the observer to <m>P</m>. Find an equation relating <m>x</m> and <m>\theta</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          Both <m>\theta</m> and <m>x</m> can be thought of as functions of time, <m>\theta(t)</m> and <m>x(t)</m>. Replace <m>\theta</m> with <m>\theta(t)</m> and <m>x</m> with <m>x(t)</m> in your equation. Each side of your equation is also a function of <m>t</m>. Call them <m>L(t)</m> (for left) and <m>R(t)</m> (for right). Take the derivatives of <m>L(t)</m> and <m>R(t)</m> with <m>\theta(t)</m> and <m>x(t)</m> treated as unspecified functions. (Your derivatives will contain <m>\theta'(t)</m> and <m>x'(t)</m>.)
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Because <m>L(t)=R(t)</m>, we must have <m>L'(t)=R'(t)</m>. Set <m>L(t)=R(t)</m> and solve for <m>\theta'(t)</m>. 
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Use your understanding of trigonometry to replace any trigonometric functions of <m>\theta(t)</m> with expressions involving <m>x(t)</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  At the time of interest, <m>x(t)=-50\text{m}</m> (since the train is <m>50\text{m}</m> west of <m>P</m>), and <m>x'(t)=+90\text{m}/\text{s}</m> (since the train is moving toward the east). Substitute these values into <m>L'(t)=R'(t)</m> and determine the value for <m>\theta'(t)</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
          </exercise>
          <exercise>
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  <xref ref="fig_elliptic"/> shows a curve called an <term>elliptic curve</term> given by the equation <m>y^{2}=x^{3}-2x-1/4</m> and a tangent line to the curve. 
                          </p>
                          <figure xml:id="fig_elliptic">
                                  <caption>An Elliptic Curve with a Tangent Line</caption>
                                  <image width="50%">
                                          <description>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  A graph of the elliptic curve <m>y^{2}=x^{3}-2x-1/4</m> with a tangent line at the point <m>(-1,\sqrt{3}/2)</m>.
                                                  </p>
                                          </description>
                                          <xi:include href="./EllipticCurve.xml"/>
                                  </image>
                          </figure>
                  </introduction>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Verify that the point <m>(-1,\sqrt{3}/2)</m> is on the elliptic curve.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  For points near <m>(-1,\sqrt{3}/2)</m>, <m>y</m> is an implicit function of <m>x</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                  </task>
          </exercise>
  </subsection>

</section>
